   717	### Closing check
   718	
   719	Appended after the item, at the end of the user message, so that the model applies it last (AGENTS §6).
   720	
   721	```text
   722	Before you return the answer, check it once. Read each evidence item back as the analyst following the company and keep it when you would write it in this week's notes because it makes some question's answer more or less likely, about the company or its customers, suppliers, competitors, regulators or the economy. Ask first whether it is new: repeated common knowledge, a long-standing situation retold and an unargued view are noise. Do not drop a reported step, figure, guidance, forecast or decision because the item retells it or another outlet reported it before: a later step compares each statement with the evidence already stored and records a repeat there. Guidance, a forecast, a plan or a decision still in force keeps its place even when retold, including a change to a plan, design or schedule that another outlet reported days or weeks before. Judge the action or information itself, not its verb tense, and do not take a statement as old because its date is missing. Fitting one of the four kinds does not keep an item. A missing amount, date or scale never drops an item on its own: a forecast, a rumour, a counter-example or a distant or conditional statement is judged like any other.
   723	These are not kept, even when a named party states them. What a row says it does not cover goes back to the test above, unless the row says it is kept:
   724	- background the analyst already knew, such as the company's history ("the company was founded in 1998"); an arrangement the item only describes as in place, even when no date is given: customers or partners that use, host, run or have built on the company's products, and the benefits they describe from that use ("two airlines fly the planemaker's jets"; "three insurers run claims on the company's software and report faster payouts"); a partnership, contract, plant or investment the item itself dates to an earlier month or year with no new step ("earlier this year the company partnered with the glassmaker on three plants"; "in May it signed a $3 billion cloud contract"); it does not cover a step the item reports as news, taken, decided, planned or under way (an order, a selection, an integration, an expansion, a deployment), even when it gives no date ("the retailer's largest supplier is opening its own stores, people familiar said this week"; "the two largest cement makers are adding kilns to meet demand"), nor a customer's, supplier's or partner's newly disclosed revenue, capacity, orders or contracted amount; a deal the item mentions as already existing is an arrangement in place even with its amount ("a rival airline already leases 40 of the same jets");
   725	- a typical claim that is common knowledge, without a figure and a specific account: that a firm leads or competes, that demand is strong or supply tight, a general risk ("if demand slows it would hurt"); a number does not lift a familiar position ("the rival grocer has led this market for years and still holds 40%"); it does not cover a dated forecast of a shortage, a price or demand ("the steelmaker expects the shortage to peak next year and last until 2028"), a competitor's newly reported share, volume or price in the company's market ("the local rival now holds about half of the regional market, worth about $5 billion"), nor a reported movement of a named price, rental rate, lead time or order book, even without a figure ("rental rates for used trucks have been climbing this quarter"); a general statement that demand stays strong or a product stays popular is not one;
   726	- a product, game, driver or benchmark specification with no business scale ("the vacuum runs 60 minutes on a charge"; "a benchmark score"), a retail price detail ("a retailer marks one model down by $50"), and a comparison the company, a partner or a third party publishes ("the maker says its drug works twice as fast as older ones"; "a reviewer's test"); it does not cover a competitor's comparison against the company's product, a partner's or customer's adoption or integration (also when a comparison shares its sentence), a reported launch, shipment, delay or cancellation of any of the company's products or a change to a product plan or configuration, including a reported sign that a planned product is going ahead ("the long-range version of the jet is likely delayed or limited to a few deliveries"; "a parts supplier's catalogue lists the unannounced model"), nor the company's decision to change the price it charges; uncertainty or a smaller product line does not make it a specification;
   727	- an unargued view or a quote that only characterises ("the stock is a hold"; "a pivotal moment"; "an industry-leading platform"), and a named analyst's reading of an event already recorded that adds no new fact or calculation ("the recall shows quality risks are rising"); it does not cover a reading that adds a new fact or calculation, nor the company's own statement that its plan, schedule or guidance still stands ("the carmaker said the launch date has not moved"), which is judged as the plan itself would be; a target price or valuation of the company is not kept, even with its assumptions and derivation, while the estimates it rests on are judged on their own (an analysts' earnings forecast is kept);
   728	- a political or strategic stance with no figure and no decision ("diversifying suppliers will not reduce dependence"); it does not cover a policy or regulatory step, nor a decision still in force that the item reports;
   729	- a share-price move, an investor's position or trade, or the market value ("shares fell 13% this month"; "he is short the stock"); it does not cover the news behind the move;
   730	- another company's matter that touches none of the company's markets (its valuation, share price, governance or a deal outside them), or that sits next to the company only by sharing an article, an event or an ecosystem; you supply the connection as the analyst and the item need not state it: a customer's spending, orders or backlog bears on demand, a supplier's capacity, output, prices or revenue on supply and input costs, a rival's products, prices or share in the company's markets on competition ("a large customer's order backlog doubled"; "the main supplier's quarterly revenue jumped on demand for the part");
   731	- the same fact said again in other words within this item: one deal, hire, programme or reported measure told in several sentences, with its terms, figures and growth rates, is one evidence item; keep the fuller wording ("a $2 billion order for 120 stores in 15 countries over three years"; "cloud revenue of $8 billion, up 30% on the year and 6% on the quarter, 40% of the total"); a deal one party signs under a wider programme, with its own amount or capacity, is its own evidence item, and a fact another outlet reported before is not a repeat within this item;
   732	- company boilerplate, bylines, forward-looking-statement text.
   733	A report and another party's denial or confirmation of it are two items, each kept only if it passes the test above; an estimate stays apart from any valuation drawn from it, and the valuation is not kept; one party's statement keeps its own planned or rumoured details.
   734	Return only the evidence items you keep.
   735	Then check the theses and links. A new thesis that asks the same underlying question, about the same subject and scope, as an open thesis or another new thesis is not proposed: link its evidence to that thesis. A new thesis needs at least one kept evidence item, and a vague or noisy topic with no outcome to watch is not proposed. Each kept evidence item links to every thesis it informs and to no thesis its own relevance says it does not inform.
   736	For each new thesis, check its opens_as, settled_by, closest_open_thesis and why_a_separate_bet: drop the thesis if it fits none of the four reasons in section 3, if no public material will settle it on a date, if an open thesis exists and its answer cannot move apart from it, or if it falls under a 'do not open' item in section 3; then link its evidence to every open thesis it informs, or leave it without links.
   737	An item whose only home would be a new thesis that is not a big matter is returned with empty links and kept without links; an item an open thesis covers is linked there, not left without links. Then read back each statement you return with empty links that states the company's own figures: return it only if a figure can change what an analyst expects of the company's revenue, profitability, funding or credit risk, or competitive position; for example, a cash-rich company's balance or cash flow that the item ties to nothing usually cannot; return only what passes.
   738	```
